## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of conditional Terraform blocks, ensuring safe incremental changes and plan stability when toggling a feature flag.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic blocks in Terraform
- Conditional resource configuration
- AWS S3 server-side encryption basics
- Plan drift and backward compatibility

## Code Example
```javascript
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "b" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  dynamic "server_side_encryption_configuration" {
    for_each = var.enable_sse ? [1] : []
    content {
      rule {
        apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
          sse_algorithm = "AES256"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support SSE with a customer-managed key (KMS) via kms_master_key_id?
- What tests would you add to validate idempotency when toggling the flag across environments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience with governance, cross-repo data sharing, and policy-as-code in real fintech environments.

## Key Concepts
- cost-governance patterns
- terraform_remote_state data source
- policy as code (OPA)
- CI gating of plans

## Code Example
```javascript
# Minimal budget fetch (pseudo-terraform)
data "terraform_remote_state" "budget" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "gov-budgets";
    key    = "costs/${var.env}.tfstate";
    region = "us-east-1";
  }
}
locals {
  monthly_budget = data.terraform_remote_state.budget.outputs["monthly_budget"]
}
```
```javascript
# Minimal OPA policy sketch
package terraform.costs
deny[msg] {
  planned_cost := input.planned_cost
  budget := input.monthly_budget
  planned_cost > budget
  msg = sprintf("Budget exceeded: %v > %v", planned_cost, budget)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to handle multi-region budgets?
- How would you add tests to verify gating behavior in CI?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your expertise in multi-account AWS infrastructure management using Terraform, testing your ability to handle complex scenarios involving provider configuration, cross-account networking, state management, policy enforcement, and automated testing.

## Key Concepts
- **Provider aliases**: Enable managing multiple AWS accounts within the same Terraform configuration
- **assume_role**: Secure cross-account access using IAM role assumption
- **remote_state**: Access outputs from other Terraform state files
- **Cross-account VPC peering**: Establish network connectivity between VPCs in different AWS accounts
- **Policy as code**: Implement governance using Sentinel or OPA policies
- **CI testing**: Validate infrastructure changes using Terratest

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "shared"
  region = "us-east-1"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/TerraformRole"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "shared_vpc" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "shared-infrastructure-state"
    key    = "vpc/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc_peering_connection" "prod_to_shared" {
  provider        = aws.prod
  vpc_id        = aws_vpc.prod.id
  peer_vpc_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.shared_vpc.outputs.vpc_id
  peer_account_id = data.terraform_remote_state.shared_vpc.outputs.account_id
  auto_accept   = false
  tags = {
    Name        = "prod-to-shared-peering"
    CostCenter  = "engineering"
    Environment = "production"
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc_peering_connection_accepter" "shared_accepter" {
  provider                  = aws.shared
  vpc_peering_connection_id = aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.id
  auto_accept               = true
  tags = {
    Name        = "shared-accept-prod-peering"
    CostCenter  = "engineering"
  }
}

# Sentinel policy example
policy "vpc_peering_policy" {
  description = "Enforce DNS resolution and CostCenter tagging for VPC peering"

  enforce {
    condition = all {
      aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.enable_dns_support
      contains(aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.tags["CostCenter"], "engineering")
    }
  }
}
```

## CI Test (Terratest)
```go
tfunc TestVPCCrossAccountPeering(t *testing.T) {
  terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
    TerraformDir: "./",
    Vars: map[string]interface{}{
      "environment": "test",
    },
  }

  planOutput := terraform.InitAndPlan(t, terraformOptions)

  assert.Contains(t, planOutput, "aws_vpc_peering_connection")
  assert.NotContains(t, planOutput, "aws_security_group")
  assert.NotContains(t, planOutput, "aws_security_group_rule")

  planJSON := terraform.PlanJson(t, terraformOptions)

  peeringResources := planJSON.Configuration.RootModule.Resources
  assert.Equal(t, 1, len(peeringResources))

  for _, resource := range peeringResources {
    assert.Equal(t, "aws_vpc_peering_connection", resource.Type)
  }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates enterprise-grade Terraform practices with proper separation of concerns, governance, and automated validation.
